# Styling: Mantine + Tailwind

Two styling systems in one app only work when each has a job and they share one set of tokens.

## Who styles what

| Tool | Job |
| --- | --- |
| **Mantine theme** | Global decisions: colours, radius, fonts, spacing scale, component defaults and variants |
| **Mantine components and props** | Controls, overlays, inputs, feedback. `variant`, `size`, `c`, `gap` and friends |
| **Tailwind utilities** | Layout and spacing on your own elements: `flex`, `grid`, `gap-4`, `md:grid-cols-3`, `min-w-0` |
| **CSS Modules** | Anything with selectors or state: restyling Mantine parts, hover of a child, data-attribute states |

Do not:

- use Tailwind classes to restyle the inside of a Mantine component — use `classNames` with a module;
- use `style={{ … }}` for static values. Inline style is for values computed at runtime (a chart colour from data, a measured width);
- write `!important`. Needing it means the layer order below is not set up;
- write a hex, rgb or px literal for colour, radius or spacing in a component.

## Tokens: three tiers

```css
/* styles/tokens.css */
:root {
  /* 1. Primitives — raw scales. Never used by a component. */
  --gray-50: #fafafa;
  --gray-900: #171717;
  --brand-600: #1e6b49;
}
:root,
[data-mantine-color-scheme='light'] {
  /* 2. Semantic — the only tier components consume. */
  --bg-canvas: var(--gray-50);
  --bg-surface: #fff;
  --text-primary: var(--gray-900);
  --border-default: #e5e5e5;
  --accent: var(--brand-600);
  --status-danger: #b42318;
}
[data-mantine-color-scheme='dark'] {
  --bg-canvas: #0b0f0d;
  --bg-surface: #121815;
  /* …every semantic token is redefined for dark */
}
/* 3. Component tokens — only where a component genuinely deviates. */
```

- A component that needs a colour that does not exist gets a **new semantic token**, not a literal.
- The dark palette is designed, not inverted. Check contrast for both.
- Name tokens for their role (`--text-secondary`), not their look (`--gray-600`).

## One source, three consumers

The semantic tokens feed Mantine, Tailwind and your CSS, so `bg-surface`, `var(--bg-surface)` and a Mantine `Paper` are always the same colour.

```ts
// theme.ts
export const theme = createTheme({ primaryColor: 'brand', colors: { brand }, fontFamily, defaultRadius: 'sm' });

// Point Mantine's own variables at the semantic tier instead of overriding components afterwards.
export const cssVariablesResolver: CSSVariablesResolver = () => ({
  variables: { '--mantine-primary-color-filled': 'var(--accent)' },
  light: { '--mantine-color-body': 'var(--bg-canvas)', '--mantine-color-text': 'var(--text-primary)' },
  dark: { '--mantine-color-body': 'var(--bg-canvas)', '--mantine-color-text': 'var(--text-primary)' },
});
```

## Cascade order (Tailwind 4)

```css
/* styles/index.css — the first CSS the app loads */
@layer theme, base, mantine, components, utilities;

@import 'tailwindcss/theme.css' layer(theme);
@import 'tailwindcss/preflight.css' layer(base);
@import '@mantine/core/styles.layer.css';
@import 'tailwindcss/utilities.css' layer(utilities);
@import './tokens.css';

@custom-variant dark (&:where([data-mantine-color-scheme='dark'], [data-mantine-color-scheme='dark'] *));

/* Utilities read the semantic tokens: `bg-surface` is var(--bg-surface). */
@theme inline {
  --color-canvas: var(--bg-canvas);
  --color-surface: var(--bg-surface);
  --color-accent: var(--accent);
}

/* One breakpoint scale: Tailwind's `md:` must mean what Mantine's `md` means. */
@theme {
  --breakpoint-*: initial;
  --breakpoint-xs: 36em;
  --breakpoint-sm: 48em;
  --breakpoint-md: 62em;
  --breakpoint-lg: 75em;
  --breakpoint-xl: 88em;
}
```

- Import every Mantine package's `styles.layer.css`, never `styles.css`. The layered files are what make the order above work.
- Resulting priority, lowest to highest: preflight → Mantine → Tailwind utilities → CSS Modules (unlayered) → inline style. A utility can adjust a Mantine component's margin; a module class beats both.
- **Tailwind 3** has no native layers: set `corePlugins: { preflight: false }`, `darkMode: ['selector', '[data-mantine-color-scheme="dark"]']`, and map `theme.extend.colors` and `theme.screens` to the same variables and breakpoints.
- PostCSS runs `postcss-preset-mantine` and `postcss-simple-vars` (Mantine's breakpoint variables), which give modules `light-dark()`, `rem()`, `@mixin hover` and `@mixin smaller-than`.

## Styling a Mantine component — in this order

1. **Theme**, when it should apply everywhere:
   ```ts
   components: { Button: Button.extend({ defaultProps: { radius: 'sm' }, classNames: buttonClasses }) }
   ```
2. **Props** for what the component already supports: `variant`, `size`, `color`, `c="dimmed"`, `gap="md"`.
3. **`classNames` + CSS Module** for structural changes, using the data attributes Mantine sets:
   ```css
   /* NavItem.module.css */
   .root {
     color: var(--text-secondary);

     &[data-active] { color: var(--accent); }
     @mixin hover { background: var(--bg-subtle); }
   }
   ```
4. **`styles` prop** — last resort. It is inline, cannot express `:hover` or data states, and is recreated on every render.

A new visual variant is `data-variant='danger'` handled in the theme's `classNames`, not a wrapper with a `switch` that returns style objects.

## Layout

For any one element, layout comes from Mantine (`Stack`, `Group`, `Grid`, `SimpleGrid`, `AppShell`) **or** from Tailwind (`flex gap-4`). Do not put Tailwind layout classes on a Mantine layout component that already controls the same property.

- Mobile-first. Responsive via Tailwind prefixes or Mantine responsive props (`cols={{ base: 1, md: 3 }}`).
- Show and hide with `hiddenFrom` / `visibleFrom` or CSS, not `useMediaQuery`, which flashes on first paint.
- Sizes come from the scale. An arbitrary value (`text-[13px]`, `mt-[7px]`) is a missing token.
- Compose conditional classes with `clsx`, not template strings.

## Colour scheme

- Mantine owns the scheme: `defaultColorScheme="auto"`, `useMantineColorScheme()` to switch. Do not write a second theme context beside it.
- Because semantic tokens flip with the scheme, most components need no dark-specific rule. Where one is needed, use `light-dark(a, b)` in a module or `dark:` in Tailwind.
- Server-rendered apps add `ColorSchemeScript` to `<head>` to avoid a flash.

## Details

- Numbers in tables and stats: `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums`.
- Animate `transform` and `opacity` only, and honour `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Focus rings stay visible, and `--border-focus` is a token.
- Self-host fonts, or use `next/font`.

## Smells to fix on sight

- `style={{ color: 'var(--text-primary)' }}` repeated across files — that is a class.
- A second Tailwind config, or a config nothing imports.
- `@mantine/*` packages on different versions.
- A colour that exists only in one component file.
- `!important` anywhere.
